WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.452
Serialization format choice should follow
the workflow.

2
00:00:03.532 --> 00:00:07.629
Common formats include JSON, XML, YAML,
protocol buffers, Avro, CSV, form data,

3
00:00:07.709 --> 00:00:12.228
Java native serialization, custom binary
formats, and framework-specific encodings.

4
00:00:12.308 --> 00:00:16.706
Data-only formats are generally easier to
constrain because they describe fields and

5
00:00:16.786 --> 00:00:17.208
values.

6
00:00:17.288 --> 00:00:21.506
Formats or configurations that reconstruct
arbitrary object graphs are harder to

7
00:00:21.586 --> 00:00:24.538
reason about and usually require much
stronger controls.

8
00:00:24.618 --> 00:00:28.414
Trust boundaries exist between clients,
APIs, partners, queues, caches,

9
00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:32.772
session stores, files, databases, object
stores, internal services, admin tools,

10
00:00:32.852 --> 00:00:34.237
and Java application code.

11
00:00:34.317 --> 00:00:37.872
Data can become untrusted when it crosses
a network, comes from a user,

12
00:00:37.952 --> 00:00:41.266
is read from a file, is pulled from a
queue, is restored from cache,

13
00:00:41.346 --> 00:00:43.093
or is supplied by another service.

14
00:00:43.173 --> 00:00:47.090
A value stored in a database can still be
untrusted if it originally came from a

15
00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:48.736
user or external integration.

16
00:00:48.816 --> 00:00:52.371
A useful review question is simple: who
created this serialized data,

17
00:00:52.451 --> 00:00:56.186
what format is it in, what code parses it,
what objects or DTOs are created,

18
00:00:56.266 --> 00:00:57.532
and what action follows?

19
00:00:57.612 --> 00:01:01.769
A public request DTO should not have the
same assumptions as an internal migration

20
00:01:01.849 --> 00:01:02.150
file.

21
00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:06.267
A queue message that changes account state
should not have the same controls as a

22
00:01:06.347 --> 00:01:07.853
low-impact analytics event.

23
00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:11.307
Security requirements should attach to
each serialization path,

24
00:01:11.387 --> 00:01:12.833
not only to the library name.

25
00:01:12.913 --> 00:01:15.685
When the application only needs a small
request shape,

26
00:01:15.765 --> 00:01:19.802
it usually does not need to deserialize a
rich domain object or arbitrary class.

27
00:01:19.882 --> 00:01:22.352
When a queue message describes a business
event,

28
00:01:22.432 --> 00:01:24.240
it should have a schema and version.

29
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.597
When an admin tool imports data, it should
have clear authorization and validation.

30
00:01:28.677 --> 00:01:30.666
Format choice, parser configuration,

31
00:01:30.746 --> 00:01:34.180
and object mapping should all express the
intended trust boundary.
